text:
Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

match a source of validity evidence to the inference it backs;
read a multitrait–multimethod matrix with Campbell and Fiske’s comparisons, and say what a failed comparison means;
compute criterion and incremental validity, and bound a validity coefficient by reliability;
say how selection shrinks a validity coefficient, correct it with Thorndike’s case II, and state what the correction assumes;
compute sensitivity, specificity and the positive predictive value, and say why the base rate and an independent criterion matter.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

say what is validated: an interpretation of scores for a use;
lay out an interpretation/use argument (scoring, generalization, extrapolation, decision) and name the evidence each link needs;
say what a reliability coefficient licenses and what it doesn’t;
recognize a criterion that borrows from the test, and say what agreement with it backs;
explain why a validity argument is never finished.

Core ideas

What gets validated?
The Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing define validity as “the degree to which evidence and theory support the interpretations of test scores for proposed uses of tests” (AERA, APA & NCME, 2014, p. 11). The wording is the point: what is supported is an interpretation, for a use.
Why does the use matter? Take a hypothetical state that gives the SAT to every junior. For admissions, scores are read as readiness for college work, and the evidence that matters is whether they predict how admitted students do. To rank the state’s schools, the same scores are averaged and read as a measure of what schools teach; now what matters is whether a school’s average reflects the school rather than whom it enrols. Evidence for the first use says little about the second.






Recall from What is measurement?: the first item on the list of what we want from a measure was insensitivity to attributes other than the target, so that a mathematics test isn’t also a test of reading English.



Where does that desideratum live? In the argument for a use. The Standards call the threat construct-irrelevant variance, with the same example of a mathematics test that leans on reading (AERA, APA & NCME, 2014, pp. 12–13). Insensitivity to reading is one claim the argument for using the scores with students learning English has to back, and Differential item functioning tests it item by item.
Is validity, then, a property of the test or of what we do with its scores? The answer rests on an assumption. If the attribute exists and differences in it produce differences in responses, validity can be read as a fact about the test, whatever the scores are used for; Borsboom, Mellenbergh and van Heerden (2004) develop that view, and Validity as causation takes it up. If instead what we can check is always a claim made with the scores, for particular respondents and a particular purpose, then evidence for one use need not carry over to another. The Standards take the second view, and advise against speaking of a test’s validity without saying which interpretation and use are meant (p. 11).
Whichever view you hold about what validity is, I don’t describe a measure as “validated”. I say which interpretation, for which use and which respondents, the evidence supports. The label travels further than the evidence: a scale studied in one population for one purpose is soon used in another, and “validated” doesn’t say whether the evidence came along.


Code
quiz({
  q: "A publisher's manual says: *The XYZ Reading Inventory is a validated measure (test–retest r = 0.88).* What is missing?",
  options: [
    {text: "The interpretation and use that the evidence is meant to support, and for whom", correct: true, why: "Evidence supports a claim about what scores mean for some purpose. Without the claim, there is no way to judge whether the evidence is enough."},
    {text: "Nothing: a test–retest correlation of 0.88 shows the inventory is valid", correct: false, why: "A retest correlation shows that scores are stable. That is one link in an argument (more on this below), not the whole of it."},
    {text: "A correlation with an established reading test", correct: false, why: "That would be useful evidence for some interpretations, but it still has to be evidence *for* a stated interpretation and use."}
  ]
})









From types of validity to construct validity






Recall from Constructs and construct maps: a construct is an attribute we postulate, given meaning by Cronbach and Meehl’s sentence linking the attribute, a situation and a probable response. Because we propose it, we could be wrong about it.



The idea that a test “has” a validity is older. The American Psychological Association’s committee on psychological tests (1950–54) set out four types of validity, each established by its own kind of study: predictive, concurrent, content and construct validity (Cronbach & Meehl, 1955, p. 281). Construct validity, the committee’s new term, was needed whenever no criterion and no body of content was accepted as fully defining the attribute (p. 282).
Two points in their paper eroded the separation. A criterion is itself a measure, and sometimes no more valid than the test it checks (p. 284). And a construct makes predictions, so a sound finding against them counts against construct and test together (p. 299): construct validity demands evidence that could have come out the other way. Messick (1995) made construct validity the whole of validity, with content, criterion relations and consequences as aspects of it. The Standards follow him, listing sources of validity evidence rather than types of validity (AERA, APA & NCME, 2014, pp. 13–14). The five sources are test content, response processes, internal structure, relations to other variables, and consequences of testing.
The first three are internal evidence, about the test and its responses, and much of this course; the last two are external, about how scores line up with the rest of what we know about respondents. How do we decide which evidence a particular use needs?


The argument
How do we get from responses to a decision? Kane (1992) proposed treating the route as an argument, and Kane (2013) calls it the interpretation/use argument. It is a chain of inferences, each resting on an assumption (its warrant) that evidence (its backing) must support. Four links are usual (Cook, Brydges, Ginsburg & Hatala, 2015):

Scoring: from responses to a score. Are the items keyed correctly, and do respondents engage with them as intended?
Generalization: from this score to the score over other occasions, forms or raters.
Extrapolation: from the test to the attribute, or performance in the world, that we care about.
Decision: from the score to an action (Cook et al. call this link implications). Does acting on the score do what it should, at acceptable cost?

The argument is laid out before data are collected, for triage: evidence should go to the assumptions most in doubt for this use (Cook et al., 2015), and more ambitious claims need more backing (Kane, 2013). The sources of evidence attach to the links: content and response processes mostly back scoring, internal structure (including reliability) backs generalization, relations to other variables back extrapolation, and consequences back the decision. Cook and Beckman (2006, Table 2) give examples of each source for four kinds of instrument.

Try it: build an argument
Pick a use. For each link, choose the source of evidence you would gather to back it.


Code
va_sources = ["Test content", "Response processes", "Internal structure", "Relations to other variables", "Consequences of testing"]
va_links = ["Scoring", "Generalization", "Extrapolation", "Decision"]
va_ok = ({Scoring: ["Test content", "Response processes"], Generalization: ["Internal structure"],
          Extrapolation: ["Relations to other variables", "Test content"], Decision: ["Consequences of testing", "Relations to other variables"]})
va_uses = ({
  "Admitting students to college (SAT)": {
    Scoring: "Answers are keyed correctly, and a scale score means the same whichever form a student took.",
    Generalization: "A student would score about the same on another form or another test date.",
    Extrapolation: "Scores reflect readiness for first-year college work.",
    Decision: "Admitting on scores, with other information, selects students who do well, and does so comparably for different groups.",
    weakest: "extrapolation and decision: whether scores predict college performance, and whether they predict it equally well for different groups."},
  "Ranking the state's schools (SAT)": {
    Scoring: "The same as for admissions: correct keys, comparable forms.",
    Generalization: "A school's average would be about the same with another year's students.",
    Extrapolation: "Differences between school averages reflect what schools teach, not whom they enrol.",
    Decision: "Ranking schools on their averages improves them, without narrowing teaching to the test.",
    weakest: "extrapolation, since a school's average depends heavily on its intake. The decision link is also in doubt, which is Campbell's worry (below)."},
  "Screening K–2 children for reading difficulty": {
    Scoring: "Young children understand the tasks, and scores reflect reading skill, not unfamiliarity with a tablet.",
    Generalization: "A child flagged this week would be flagged again next week.",
    Extrapolation: "A low score signals risk of later reading difficulty.",
    Decision: "Children who are flagged get help that works, and few children who need help are missed.",
    weakest: "extrapolation: whether a flag in kindergarten predicts reading difficulty later, which takes a longitudinal study."},
  "Comparing two groups in a research study": {
    Scoring: "Items are keyed and coded the same way for everyone.",
    Generalization: "Group means are precise enough for the comparison (reliability matters less for a mean than for one respondent).",
    Extrapolation: "A difference in scores is a difference in the construct, not in some other attribute the items also touch.",
    Decision: "The study's conclusion follows from the difference.",
    weakest: "extrapolation: that the items work the same way in both groups, the question Differential item functioning takes up."}
})
viewof va_use = Inputs.radio(Object.keys(va_uses), {value: Object.keys(va_uses)[0], label: "Use"})
viewof va_pick = Inputs.form(Object.fromEntries(va_links.map(l =&gt; [l, Inputs.select(["(choose)", ...va_sources], {label: l})])))














































Code
{
  const va_cell = "padding:0.3rem 0.6rem;vertical-align:top;text-align:left";
  return htl.html`&lt;table style="font-size:0.9rem;border-collapse:collapse"&gt;
  &lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th style=${va_cell}&gt;Link&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th style=${va_cell}&gt;The claim for this use&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th style=${va_cell}&gt;Your evidence&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/thead&gt;
  &lt;tbody&gt;${va_links.map(l =&gt; {
    const p = va_pick[l], ok = va_ok[l].includes(p);
    return htl.html`&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style=${va_cell}&gt;&lt;b&gt;${l}&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style=${va_cell}&gt;${va_uses[va_use][l]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style=${va_cell}&gt;${p === "(choose)" ? "" :
      ok ? htl.html`&lt;span style="color:${palette.main}"&gt;${p}&lt;/span&gt; backs this link.` :
           htl.html`&lt;span style="color:${palette.contrast}"&gt;${p}&lt;/span&gt; mostly backs another link; here, think ${va_ok[l].map(s =&gt; s.toLowerCase()).join(" or ")}.`}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;`;
  })}&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;`;
}









Code
md`Your choices back **${va_links.filter(l =&gt; va_ok[l].includes(va_pick[l])).length} of 4** links. For this use, the link most in doubt is ${va_uses[va_use].weakest}`







The scoring and generalization claims read much the same across uses; the extrapolation and decision claims are where the two SAT uses part company. The mapping is a guide, not a rule: content evidence also backs extrapolation when the claim is that the items represent a domain, as with the blueprints in Constructs and construct maps.
Three questions are worth asking of any validity claim. What is the proposed interpretation and use? What evidence supports it? Is that evidence enough for this use?


Code
quiz({
  q: "A reading screener's technical report gives a test–retest correlation of 0.90 and nothing else. The proposed use is to flag K–2 children at risk of later reading difficulty. Which link does the evidence back?",
  options: [
    {text: "Generalization", correct: true, why: "A retest correlation says a child would get about the same score again. It says nothing about what the score predicts or what happens to flagged children."},
    {text: "Extrapolation", correct: false, why: "Extrapolation needs a relation to something outside the test, here later reading. A test can be very stable and still track something other than risk."},
    {text: "Decision", correct: false, why: "The decision link is about what flagging does: whether flagged children get help that works, and how many are missed. A correlation between two administrations can't speak to that."}
  ]
})










Reliability is necessary, not sufficient






Recall from Classical test theory and reliability: reliability is the share of observed-score variance that is true-score variance, and alpha estimates it from one administration, as a lower bound when the item errors are uncorrelated. A test’s correlation with anything is bounded by the square root of its reliability.



Where does reliability sit in the argument? At the generalization link. The bound in the recall makes it necessary: scores that don’t agree with themselves can’t agree well with anything else. Cook and Beckman (2006) give a clinical example. Three blood-pressure readings on a stable patient, within three minutes, come out at 185/100, 80/40 and 140/70. We wouldn’t average them to 135/70 and move on. But is agreement among readings enough?

Try it: three readings
A patient’s true systolic pressure is 135. Each reading adds error with the SD you choose, plus a fixed bias from a miscalibrated cuff.


Code
viewof bp_sd = Inputs.range([1, 40], {value: 30, step: 1, label: "Error SD of a reading (mmHg)"})
viewof bp_bias = Inputs.range([-30, 30], {value: 0, step: 1, label: "Cuff bias (mmHg)"})
viewof bp_n = Inputs.range([1, 12], {value: 3, step: 1, label: "Number of readings"})
viewof bp_seed = Inputs.range([1, 20], {value: 1, step: 1, label: "Visit (seed)"})
































Code
bp = {
  const r = rng(bp_seed), z = Array.from({length: 12}, () =&gt; r.norm());
  const x = z.slice(0, bp_n).map((v, i) =&gt; ({i: i + 1, y: 135 + bp_bias + bp_sd * v}));
  const m = x.reduce((u, d) =&gt; u + d.y, 0) / bp_n, se = bp_sd / Math.sqrt(bp_n);
  return {x, m, se};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: 640, height: 260, x: {label: "Reading", domain: [0.5, 12.5], ticks: 12}, y: {label: "Systolic (mmHg)", domain: [30, 240]},
  marks: [
    Plot.rect([{}], {x1: 0.5, x2: 12.5, y1: bp.m - 2 * bp.se, y2: bp.m + 2 * bp.se, fill: palette.light, fillOpacity: 0.4}),
    Plot.ruleY([135], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4,3"}),
    Plot.text([{y: 135}], {x: 12.4, y: "y", text: () =&gt; "true 135", textAnchor: "end", dy: -7, fill: palette.guide, fontSize: 10}),
    Plot.ruleY([bp.m], {stroke: palette.contrast, strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.dot(bp.x, {x: "i", y: "y", r: 5, fill: palette.main})
  ]
})


















Code
md`Average of ${bp_n} reading${bp_n &gt; 1 ? "s" : ""}: **${bp.m.toFixed(0)}**, give or take about ${(2 * bp.se).toFixed(0)} (two standard errors, the shaded band). The average is off the true value by ${(bp.m - 135).toFixed(0)}; the cuff's bias accounts for ${bp_bias} of that.`







With the error SD at 30, three readings leave the average uncertain by about ±35. Now set the error SD to 3 and the bias to 20: the readings agree closely, and every one is about 20 too high. Reliability measures agreement among readings, and a consistent bias is invisible to it; only a comparison with something outside the cuff, such as a calibrated device, finds it. That comparison is extrapolation evidence.
Back to the dog scale. Under the classical assumptions, an alpha of 0.81 says that at least about 81% of the variance in these owners’ subscale totals is true-score variance: the six ratings hang together. That backs generalization across items, for one rater. It doesn’t say the items measure inattention rather than, say, obedience, or that another rater would agree. Csibra et al. checked the second, comparing owners’ ratings with dog trainers’, and describe the agreement as fair for inattention and moderate for hyperactivity/impulsivity.


Code
quiz({
  q: "Six owner-rated inattention items have an alpha of 0.81. Which claim does that support, under the classical assumptions?",
  options: [
    {text: "At least about 81% of the variance in these owners' subscale totals is true-score variance", correct: true, why: "Alpha is a lower bound on reliability when the item errors are uncorrelated. That is a claim about consistency across items, which backs the generalization link."},
    {text: "A trainer rating the same dogs would largely agree with the owners", correct: false, why: "Alpha is about agreement among items for one rater. Agreement between raters is separate evidence; in this study it was fair to moderate."},
    {text: "The subscale measures inattention", correct: false, why: "The items could hang together because they all tap something else, such as obedience. What the total measures is an extrapolation claim, and it needs evidence from outside the scale."}
  ]
})









Try it: a criterion that borrows from the test
Agreement with a criterion is the usual backing for extrapolation. What if the criterion already contains the test? Here 1,500 simulated children answer a nine-item screener. An independent criterion (say, impairment judged by a clinician who never sees the screener) flags 15% of them. A “diagnosis” mixes that criterion’s information with the screener’s own count, putting weight w on the count. The Standards list this situation, criterion contamination, among the artifacts a study of test–criterion relations should look for (AERA, APA & NCME, 2014, Standard 11.8, p. 180).
Separation is measured by the AUC: the chance that a randomly chosen flagged child has a higher count than a randomly chosen unflagged one, ties counting half (0.5 is none, 1 is perfect; Gathering validity evidence develops it).






NoteWhat is the AUC?





Take a score and a group label (case or non-case). Pick a cut: everyone at or above it is flagged. Sensitivity is the share of cases flagged; specificity is the share of non-cases not flagged. As the cut moves from the top of the scale to the bottom, both change, and plotting sensitivity against \(1 - \text{specificity}\) traces the ROC curve, from (0, 0) to (1, 1).
The AUC is the area under that curve. It has a direct reading: for a randomly chosen case with score \(S_1\) and a randomly chosen non-case with score \(S_0\), \[\text{AUC} = \Pr(S_1 &gt; S_0) + \tfrac{1}{2}\Pr(S_1 = S_0)\] (Hanley & McNeil, 1982). An AUC of 0.5 is chance (a diagonal ROC curve), and 1 is perfect separation: every case scores above every non-case.
What it doesn’t tell you: which cut to use, since it averages over all of them, or how many flagged respondents are cases, which depends on the base rate (problem 1).





Code
viewof cc_w = Inputs.range([0, 1], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "Weight w on the screener's count"})









Code
cc_sim = simCriterion({seed: 33, n: 1500})
cc_curve = grid(0, 1, 21).flatMap(w =&gt; { const a = cc_sim.at(w); return [{w, v: a.aucDiag, what: "against the diagnosis"}, {w, v: a.aucCrit, what: "against the independent criterion"}]; })
cc_now = cc_sim.at(cc_w)
cc_hist = {
  const n1 = cc_now.d.reduce((u, v) =&gt; u + v, 0), n0 = cc_now.d.length - n1, out = [];
  for (let k = 0; k &lt;= 9; k++) {
    const a1 = cc_sim.count.filter((c, j) =&gt; c === k && cc_now.d[j]).length, a0 = cc_sim.count.filter((c, j) =&gt; c === k && !cc_now.d[j]).length;
    out.push({x1: k - 0.42, x2: k, share: a0 / n0, group: "not flagged"}, {x1: k, x2: k + 0.42, share: a1 / n1, group: "flagged by the diagnosis"});
  }
  return out;
}
cc_p1 = Plot.plot({
  width: 360, height: 270, x: {label: "Weight on the screener's count (w)"}, y: {label: "Screener's AUC", domain: [0.5, 1]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["against the diagnosis", "against the independent criterion"], range: [palette.contrast, palette.main]},
  marks: [Plot.line(cc_curve, {x: "w", y: "v", stroke: "what", strokeWidth: 2.5}), Plot.ruleX([cc_w], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4,3"})]
})
cc_p2 = Plot.plot({
  width: 340, height: 270, x: {label: "Screener count", domain: [-0.5, 9.5]}, y: {label: "Share of the group", domain: [0, 0.4]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["not flagged", "flagged by the diagnosis"], range: [palette.light, palette.contrast]},
  marks: [Plot.rectY(cc_hist, {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", y: "share", fill: "group"}), Plot.ruleY([0])]
})
htl.html`&lt;div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem;"&gt;${cc_p1}${cc_p2}&lt;/div&gt;`





















































Code
md`At w = ${cc_w.toFixed(2)}, the screener's AUC against the diagnosis is **${cc_now.aucDiag.toFixed(2)}**; against the independent criterion it is **${cc_now.aucCrit.toFixed(2)}**. The diagnosis agrees with the independent criterion for ${(100 * cc_now.agree).toFixed(0)}% of children.`







At w = 0 the screener’s AUC is about 0.76. As the weight rises the AUC against the diagnosis climbs, to 0.97 at w = 0.5 and exactly 1 at w = 1, where the diagnosis is a cut on the count and the histograms no longer overlap. The AUC against the independent criterion doesn’t move. The rising number measures how much the diagnosis borrowed, not how well the screener finds the children we care about.



A measure is never finished
Suppose an argument has been backed, link by link. Is the work done? Not for long. Campbell (1979) proposed what is now called Campbell’s law: an indicator used to make social decisions attracts pressure to corrupt it, and tends to distort the very processes it was meant to track. A test that measured what schools teach before it ranked them may measure test preparation after. Uses also shift as needs change, and the evidence has to follow (Kane, 2013). Validation is a continuing activity, not a certificate.
That leaves a tension, which we might call the validity paradox. The argument-based view asks a lot, for good reasons, but a complete argument for every use can be very hard to deliver. California’s parent guide to its state tests lists four uses of the results, the first of which is to help parents and teachers talk about a student’s performance (California Department of Education, 2015, p. 5). Backing the first would take a study of whether score reports start or improve conversations between parents and teachers, a different kind of study from any psychometric analysis (problem 4 asks you to design it). The paradox is one reason to consider a narrower account of validity, which Validity as causation takes up.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

fit an item response model to trial data, one row per trial, and say what it assumes about trials of the same item;
replace item difficulties with a function of a feature of the stimulus, and compare the two in and out of sample;
decompose the variance of trial responses into an average effect, person differences in that effect, and trial noise, and compute the reliability of a person’s effect from it;
explain why a large, universal experimental effect can come with unreliable individual differences;
say why ability estimates are biased when respondents choose their own trials, and what adjusting for trial features does about it.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

turn a raw score into a percentile rank, z score, linear T and normalized T, and say what each assumes;
put an error band around a score, from one SEM or a conditional SEM, and say when the two differ;
compute the reliability of a difference score and a reliable change threshold;
treat a cut score as a decision, do an Angoff calculation, and say why classification near a cut is uncertain;
say what a score report should carry, and why group statistics use plausible values.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

describe the diffusion model’s four parameters and simulate trials from it;
predict what a harder stimulus and a speed instruction do, and estimate the parameters with EZ-diffusion;
derive the 2PL from the diffusion model and say what discrimination means as a process;
explain why errors can be slower than correct responses, and why that has to be checked within a condition;
say when a diffusion model, or a race model, suits the data in hand.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

write the many-facet Rasch model, and say what a rater’s severity is and what the rating design must provide to estimate it;
fit the model with TAM, read a Wright map with a rater column, and adjust scores for the raters a respondent drew;
tell severity, centrality and halo apart, and say what data would show each;
explain why the hierarchical rater model gives extra ratings of one response less credit than the many-facet model does;
say when a rater is better treated as a fixed parameter, a random effect or a variance component.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

say what makes a response polytomous, and write the graded response, (generalized) partial credit and sequential models as different ways of splitting a response into binary questions;
read category response functions and the expected score curve, and say what disordered steps do and don’t mean;
explain why the sum score is sufficient under the partial credit model, and what takes its place under the other models;
compute the information in a polytomous item, and say what collapsing its categories costs and where;
fit and compare these models with mirt, and judge how much the choice matters for respondents’ scores.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

say what parameter invariance claims, and why it is a property of the model rather than of any set of estimates;
explain why two groups’ estimates sit on different scales, and derive how 2PL slopes and difficulties change with a group’s mean and SD of ability;
explain why a group drawn from a narrow range of ability gives imprecise item estimates;
explain why selecting respondents on the sum score of the same items distorts 2PL estimates, and why the Rasch model fitted by conditional maximum likelihood is not affected;
check invariance in real data with mirt, and choose a split that tests it.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

write down Bock’s nominal response model, say how its slopes order the options along \(\theta\), and say when it becomes the generalized partial credit model;
read option curves: tell a key from a falling distractor, one that draws partial knowledge, and one that rises with \(\theta\);
say where distractors add information and why, and compare an item’s information with and without them;
explain how Thissen and Steinberg’s multiple-choice model and the nested logit model describe guessing, and how that differs from the 3PL’s floor;
fit the nominal and nested logit models with mirt, and use option curves to find items that need a second look.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

classify a kind of missing response as MCAR, MAR or MNAR, and say what the long table can’t tell you;
explain why marginal maximum likelihood can ignore gaps left by a booklet design or a stopping rule, and when it can’t;
show what scoring an omit as wrong, or leaving it missing, does to one respondent’s estimate;
recover omitted and not-reached responses from item order, and check the rule against a source that records them;
fit the same data under several treatments of the gaps, including a response-propensity model, and report how far the estimates move.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

fit a multigroup CFA in lavaan and say how the two groups are put on one scale;
climb the ladder (configural, metric, scalar) with scaled difference tests and changes in CFI, and say which comparison each step supports;
find the constraint that fails with score tests, and fit and report a partial invariance model;
say what one unequal intercept does to a comparison of sum scores and of factor means;
choose an estimator for a multigroup fit, and say what ordered categories change.

Goals
By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

explain why estimating item parameters is a missing-data problem, and what it would be if \(\theta\) were known;
run joint maximum likelihood, and explain why its difficulties don’t settle on the truth when the test is short, however many respondents there are;
write the marginal likelihood on a grid of nodes, and say how direct maximization and EM climb it differently;
carry out an E step and an M step: posterior weights, expected counts, and a weighted logistic regression for each item;
